.TH GASEOUS-GUSTY-BALL 1 "October 2026" "gaseous-gusty-ball" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gaseous-gusty-ball \- computational fluid dynamics on a sphere for generating gas-giant cubemap textures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gaseous-gusty-ball
.B \-i
.I input.png
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gaseous-gusty-ball
simulates 2D incompressible fluid flow on the surface of a unit sphere, advects
colored tracer particles through the evolving velocity field, and renders their
fading, alpha-blended motion trails into six cubemap face images suitable for
planet rendering in games or animations.
.PP
At initialization, particles are distributed evenly across the sphere using a
Fibonacci spiral lattice. Each particle's permanent RGBA color is sampled from
the input PNG image via an equirectangular projection: the image width wraps
360 degrees around the equator (longitude) and the image height runs from the
north pole (top row) to the south pole (bottom row). A tall, thin image with
horizontal color variations therefore establishes latitudinal color bands on
the sphere.
.PP
The initial fluid velocity at each point on the sphere is the sum of two sources:
.IP 1. 4
Multi-octave 3D simplex curl noise evaluated on the unit sphere and projected
onto the local tangent plane, producing divergence-free whorls across a
configurable range of spatial scales.
.IP 2. 4
Counter-rotating zonal velocity bands parallel to the equator with eastward
speed
.IR "u = A * sin(n * colatitude)" ,
where
.I colatitude
runs from 0 at the north pole to pi at the south pole.
.PP
The fluid is evolved using the barotropic vorticity equation on an equiangular
cubed-sphere grid with exact spherical Voronoi finite-volume operators, a
multigrid-preconditioned conjugate gradient Poisson solver, and semi-Lagrangian
great-circle advection (with Back-and-Forth Error Compensation and Conditioning,
BFECC, enabled by default).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Input and Output Options"
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-input =\fIFILE\fR
Input PNG file used to color particles at their initial positions (required).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-outdir =\fIDIR\fR
Directory where output images are written (default:
.BR out ).
Created automatically if it does not exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix =P
Filename prefix for output images (default:
.BR gg ).
.TP
.BR \-\-layout = cross | gl
Cubemap face naming and numbering convention (default:
.BR cross ).
See
.B CUBEMAP LAYOUTS
below.
.TP
.B \-\-sequence
Write numbered frame files (e.g.,
.IR gg-00050-0.png )
instead of overwriting
.IR gg0.png " .. " gg5.png
at each output interval.
.TP
.B \-\-net
Also write an assembled 4x3 unfolded cross-net PNG image
.RI ( <prefix>-net.png )
at each output step.
.TP
.B \-\-preview
Also write a 2:1 equirectangular preview PNG image
.RI ( <prefix>-preview.png )
at each output step.
.TP
.BI \-\-res =R
Width and height in pixels of each output cubemap face (default:
.BR 1024 ).
.TP
.BR \-\-bg = transparent | \fIRRGGBB\fR
Background behind fading particle trails (default:
.BR transparent ).
When set to
.BR transparent ,
outputs 4-channel RGBA PNGs with un-premultiplied colors. When set to a
6-digit hexadecimal color (with or without a leading
.RB ' # '),
composites the trails over that solid color and writes 3-channel RGB PNGs.
.SS "Fluid and Initial Condition Options"
.TP
.BI \-\-particles =M
Number of tracer particles distributed over the sphere (default:
.BR 1000000 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-grid =N
Number of fluid grid cells along each edge of the 6 cubed-sphere faces, for a
total of
.I 6*N*N
cells (default:
.BR 256 ).
Powers of two allow full geometric multigrid preconditioning.
.TP
.BI \-\-bands =n
Number of counter-rotating latitudinal velocity bands from pole to pole
(default:
.BR 8 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-band\-amp =A
Peak velocity magnitude of the zonal bands (default:
.BR 1.0 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-noise\-amp =A
Target root-mean-square (RMS) speed of the projected curl-noise velocity field
(default:
.BR 0.5 ).
Because the field is RMS-normalized, changing scale or octave parameters does
not alter the overall eddy kinetic energy.
.TP
.BI \-\-noise\-scale =s
Base spatial frequency of the curl noise on the unit sphere (default:
.BR 3.0 ).
Smaller values produce fewer, larger planet-scale whorls; larger values produce
smaller, tighter eddies.
.TP
.BI \-\-noise\-octaves =k
Number of fractal curl-noise octaves summed into the initial velocity field
(default:
.BR 4 ;
0 disables curl noise).
.TP
.BI \-\-noise\-lacunarity =l
Frequency multiplier between successive curl-noise octaves (default:
.BR 2.0 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-noise\-gain =g
Amplitude weight multiplier between successive curl-noise octaves (default:
.BR 0.5 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-forcing =k
Relaxation rate (per unit simulation time) nudging the vorticity field back
toward the initial zonal band vorticity profile (default:
.BR 0 ,
meaning free unforced evolution). Values around
.B 0.1
to
.B 1.0
prevent bands from gradually mixing away over long simulations.
.TP
.BI \-\-rotation =W
Planetary angular velocity for the Coriolis force, advecting absolute vorticity
.I omega + 2*W*sin(latitude)
(default:
.BR 0 ).
Non-zero rotation promotes Rossby wave dynamics and persistent zonal jets.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-bfecc
Disable Back-and-Forth Error Compensation and Conditioning and use single-pass
semi-Lagrangian vorticity advection (faster, but more numerically diffusive).
.SS "Time Integration and Solver Options"
.TP
.BI \-\-dt =T
Fixed simulation time step. By default,
.B gaseous-gusty-ball
chooses the time step dynamically at each step from
.B \-\-cfl
and the maximum fluid speed.
.TP
.BI \-\-cfl =C
Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy number used for automatic time stepping (default:
.BR 1.0 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-steps =S
Total number of simulation steps to run (default:
.BR 2000 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-every =K
Write output images every
.I K
simulation steps, and on the final step (default:
.BR 50 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-cg\-tol =TOL
Relative residual tolerance for the preconditioned conjugate gradient Poisson
solver (default:
.BR 1e-4 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-cg\-maxit =IT
Maximum conjugate gradient iterations per step (default:
.BR 500 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-seed =S
Unsigned integer seed for the curl-noise permutation table (default:
.BR 1 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-threads =T
Number of OpenMP worker threads to use (default: all available logical cores).
.SS "Trail Rendering Options"
.TP
.BI \-\-fade =f
Fraction of existing trail opacity removed per simulation step, in the range
[0, 1) (default:
.BR 0.02 ).
Smaller values produce longer, more persistent trails; larger values produce
shorter trails.
.TP
.BI \-\-trail\-alpha =a
Opacity of each individual particle splat deposit, in the range (0, 1]
(default:
.BR 0.35 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-splat\-radius =r
Half-width of the tent-filter splat kernel in output face texels (default:
.BR 1.0 ;
clamped to at least 0.75).
.SS "Help"
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of command-line options and exit.
.SH CUBEMAP LAYOUTS
The sphere uses a coordinate system with
.B +Y
at the north pole,
.B \-Y
at the south pole,
.B +Z
at longitude 0 on the equator, and
.B +X
at 90 degrees east.
.SS "cross layout (default)"
Writes six files named
.IR <prefix>0.png " through " <prefix>5.png
(or
.IR <prefix>-NNNNN-0.png " .. " <prefix>-NNNNN-5.png
with
.BR \-\-sequence )
arranged as an unfolded cube net viewed from outside the planet:
.PP
.nf
          +------+
          |  4   |
          | (+Y) |
   +------+------+------+------+
   |  0   |  1   |  2   |  3   |
   | (+Z) | (+X) | (\-Z) | (\-X) |
   +------+------+------+------+
          |  5   |
          | (\-Y) |
          +------+
.fi
.PP
Faces 0, 1, 2, and 3 run eastward around the equator with north (+Y) pointing
up. Face 4 is the north pole (its bottom edge joins the top edge of face 0) and
face 5 is the south pole (its top edge joins the bottom edge of face 0).
.SS "gl layout"
Writes six files named
.IR <prefix>-posx.png ,
.IR <prefix>-negx.png ,
.IR <prefix>-posy.png ,
.IR <prefix>-negy.png ,
.IR <prefix>-posz.png ,
and
.IR <prefix>-negz.png
following the standard OpenGL cubemap face naming.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate a set of 1024x1024 cubemap faces and an equirectangular preview from
an input color strip, saving intermediate frames every 100 steps:
.PP
.nf
    gaseous-gusty-ball \-i test/bands.png \-o out \-\-steps 1000 \-\-every 100 \\
        \-\-sequence \-\-preview \-\-net
.fi
.PP
Simulate a planet with 12 bands, larger initial vortices, persistent jet
forcing, Coriolis rotation, and a dark solid background:
.PP
.nf
    gaseous-gusty-ball \-i test/bands.png \-o out \-\-bands 12 \-\-noise\-scale 1.8 \\
        \-\-forcing 0.3 \-\-rotation 10 \-\-bg 080810
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libpng (3)
